From: Try Omarchy contributors
Subject: [PATCH] cocoa: full screen beside the camera notch

Applies after the other Try Omarchy Cocoa patches.

On a MacBook with a camera notch, AppKit's full screen puts the window below
the housing and leaves the strip beside it to the menu bar, painted black by
the window server's menu bar window (level 24) even when the menu bar is
hidden. The guest is told where the housing is (the try-omarchy package
describes it in Omarchy's display-cutouts.json, so Omarchy's own bar grows
past it), so the VM can use the whole panel. Public AppKit only:

- QemuWindow keeps the display's whole frame where AppKit's full-screen tile
  stops below the strip, and leaves it unconstrained there.
- While it covers the strip and has the keyboard, the window sits at
  NSStatusWindowLevel, just above the menu bar's black strip. An alert, a
  menu, another app taking the keyboard and the way out of full screen put
  it back at the normal level.
- Non-immersive full screen keeps macOS's auto-hidden menu bar and Dock: the
  window steps down while the pointer is at the top edge or the Dock's edge
  and comes back once it moves on past them.
- The guest is offered the whole display's size, so it follows the window.

Screens without a housing (external displays, Macs without a notch, the
built-in panel's modes below the camera) report no safe-area inset and keep
QEMU's behaviour. TRYOMARCHY_NOTCH=0 keeps full screen below the housing
everywhere. QEMU's log says when full screen covers the strip.

